<article>
<header class="hero"><div class="shell"><a href="/guides/">← All guides</a><p class="eyebrow">Nashville buyer guide</p><h1>{title}</h1><p class="dek">A higher national mortgage rate doesn't necessarily mean that every home comes with the same financing options.</p><p class="byline">By Jason Kaczmarski · October 14, 2026 · 4 minute read</p></div></header>
<div class="shell body">
<p class="lead">The mortgage-rate headlines aren't encouraging. But buyers shopping for new construction around Nashville may have another option worth exploring: builder-paid financing incentives.</p>
<p>On October 8, 2026, Freddie Mac reported a <strong>7.40% national average 30-year fixed mortgage rate</strong>, compared with 7.28% one week earlier and 6.30% a year earlier. That was the highest weekly figure since November 2023—nearly three years. It's a benchmark, not a quote any individual borrower is guaranteed. <a href="https://freddiemac.gcs-web.com/news-releases/news-release-details/mortgage-rates-average-740">Source: Freddie Mac</a>.</p>
<h2>Some builders are helping buyers lower their rate</h2>
<p>New-home builders have inventory to sell, and financing incentives can be a way to compete without reducing every home's asking price. On select homes, some Middle Tennessee builders advertise a lower mortgage rate through a preferred lender, a contribution toward discount points, or temporary payment assistance.</p>
<p>These offers can be meaningful, but the fine print matters. A headline such as “2.99%” might refer to a temporary introductory rate rather than a 30-year fixed rate. A rate advertised for one quick-move-in home may not apply to another house in the same subdivision. Availability and loan qualifications can change without notice.</p>
<h2>Here's how much the rate matters</h2>
<p>On a $400,000 mortgage, assuming a 30-year fixed loan at each example rate, principal and interest would be approximately:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Interest rate</th><th>Monthly principal &amp; interest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7.40%</td><td>$2,769</td></tr><tr><td>5.99%</td><td>$2,395</td></tr><tr><td>4.99%</td><td>$2,145</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><small>Illustrative calculations only, not current loan offers. They assume equal loan amounts and fully fixed 30-year terms. Taxes, insurance, HOA fees, mortgage insurance, points and other charges aren't included. Temporary buydowns work differently.</small></p>
<p>That's why I think it's worth asking about the financing instead of judging new construction by the list price alone.</p>
<h2>What to verify before you fall in love with the offer</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Is the rate fixed for all 30 years?</strong> Or is it a temporary 1-0 or 2-1 buydown?</li><li><strong>Which specific homes qualify?</strong> Incentives often apply to certain completed homes or require a contract or closing by a deadline.</li><li><strong>What is the true cost?</strong> Compare the full Loan Estimate, APR, discount points, lender fees and cash needed to close.</li><li><strong>Is a preferred lender required?</strong> Compare with an outside lender, too.</li><li><strong>Can you combine incentives?</strong> The lowest rate may not be available alongside the biggest price reduction or other discounts.</li></ul>
<p>For comparison, a resale seller can sometimes offer a credit toward a mortgage buydown, too. Whether you're comparing new construction and existing homes—or two different builder communities—look at the entire transaction. <a href="/guides/nashville-seller-concessions-2026/">Here's my buyer guide to concessions and rate buydowns.</a></p>
<h2>I'll help you find the homes actually offering incentives</h2>
<p>There isn't one reliable rate offer that applies to every builder or every neighborhood. What matters is finding available homes in your price range and verifying their current financing terms.</p>
<section class="cta" id="request-builder-incentives" aria-labelledby="request-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">Request your personalized list</p><h2 id="request-title">Want a list of homes with builder rate incentives?</h2><p>Share your preferred area and budget. I'll email you available Nashville-area homes with builder-offered financing incentives to explore.</p></div>
<form class="incentive-form" action="https://formsubmit.co/jason@jasonknashville.com" method="POST">
